# Line-ending policy
#
# scripts/sync-script-docs.mjs generates Markdown with LF line endings, and
# `docs:scripts:check` (run in `prebuild`) compares that generated output
# against the working copy. On Windows, core.autocrlf=true checks Markdown out
# as CRLF, so the verifier reports a false "out of sync". Pinning Markdown to
# LF keeps the working copy, the committed blob, and the generator in agreement
# on every platform.
*.md text eol=lf

# Golden serializer fixtures (src/app/golden-workspace.test.ts) pin the
# byte-exact output of the dual-use storage serializers. They must never be
# touched by line-ending conversion — `-text` makes git treat them as binary
# for eol purposes, so the working copy always matches the committed blob
# byte-for-byte on every platform. (This also overrides the `*.md eol=lf`
# rule above for the .md fixture.)
src/app/__fixtures__/golden-workspace.csv -text
src/app/__fixtures__/golden-workspace.md -text

# The operating-guide builtin is emitted by scripts/gen-operating-guide.mjs,
# which normalizes its output to LF. Pin the committed + working copy to LF so
# core.autocrlf can't check it out as CRLF and make `prebuild`'s regeneration
# see phantom line-ending drift against the freshly generated LF file.
src/app/operating-guide-builtin.generated.ts text eol=lf

# The desktop/ subtree is a new Electron wrapper, not part of src/app's CRLF
# working-tree convention (which core.autocrlf governs alone, with no
# .gitattributes entry of its own). Pin it to LF so it matches the rest of the
# LF-pinned tooling (scripts/, docs/) rather than inheriting CRLF by accident.
desktop/** text eol=lf

# Windows installer assets are consumed BYTE-EXACTLY by NSIS and by Windows
# itself: the sidebar must stay a 164x314 24-bit BMP and the icon a multi-size
# ICO. Git would normally infer "binary" from a NUL byte in the first 8000, but
# that is a heuristic over image DATA, not a guarantee — and a single spurious
# CRLF conversion corrupts the file with no error at commit, build or install
# time. `-text` states it outright, the same way the golden fixtures above do.
*.ico -text
*.bmp -text

# This banner carries a C2PA content-credentials manifest, and a C2PA claim is a
# hash over the file's BYTES with the manifest's own range excluded -- so any
# line-ending conversion invalidates it. Measured 2026-09-10: committing it under
# core.autocrlf=true stripped 66 CR bytes (10534 -> 10468), i.e. the repo copy
# would not have validated on a non-CRLF checkout. `-text` stores it verbatim.
#
# Only this file is pinned, not *.svg: every other SVG here is hand-edited LF
# source that SHOULD stay normalised. Pin any further credentialed asset the
# same way, individually, and re-check with:
#   git cat-file blob :<path> | cmp - <path>
public/ai-pm-cockpit-banner.svg -text
#
# ★★ The splash asset below is credentialed too, but the damage runs the OTHER
# WAY and the banner's reason above does not transfer. Measured 2026-09-10: it
# holds ZERO CR bytes, so `git add` strips nothing -- it is CHECKOUT that breaks
# it, because autocrlf=true rewrites an LF text file to CRLF in the working
# tree. Unpinned it would land as `i/lf w/crlf` (the state every e2e/*.ts is
# in), i.e. the file on disk would no longer be the bytes the credential was
# signed over, while the blob stayed valid. Pinned it reads `i/lf w/lf
# attr/-text`. Verify with `git ls-files --eol <path>`, and the blob with the
# cmp above -- the cmp ALONE cannot see this direction, since it compares the
# blob against the very working copy a bad checkout would have rewritten.
public/splash-aipm-cockpit.svg -text
